WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:33.917
Ask for a stable name. The caller requests the payment capability by service name instead of storing an individual pod address that may disappear during the next deployment.

2
00:00:33.917 --> 00:01:07.250
Maintain endpoints. The platform or registry tracks service destinations and readiness. Registration and removal need reliable ownership and observable convergence.

3
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:48.583
Choose a destination. Client or server routing selects a usable endpoint. Discovery and load balancing cooperate, but they describe different responsibilities.

4
00:01:48.583 --> 00:02:20.917
Replace an instance. An old instance becomes unready and a replacement joins. Cached addresses and existing connections can outlive the registry update.

5
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:49.250
Retry within a budget. A failed connection may use a safe bounded retry against current membership. Do not hide repeated errors with unlimited retries.

6
00:02:49.250 --> 00:03:30.917
Verify service identity. Knowing an address does not authorize a request. Validate the relevant identity, transport security and resource permissions at the appropriate boundaries.
